{{also|Chikan}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|ja|痴漢}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|chikan|s}}

# {{lb|en|in Japan}} Any act of public molestation or offense, usually of a sexual nature, against unsuspecting victims.
# {{lb|en|in Japan}} A person, usually a man, who [[rub]]s against or [[grope]]s others, usually women, in [[crowd]]s, often in trains, to attain [[sexual]] [[pleasure]].

===Usage notes===
Most users italicize this word, indicating that it is usually considered a Japanese word. However, its unitalicized use and the use of the plural form "chickans" indicate that it is making the transition from a quoted foreign word to a borrowed English word.

===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia|Chikan (embroidery)}}
From {{bor|en|hi|चिकन}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A form of [[Indian]] [[cotton]] [[embroidery]] that is associated with the [[Lucknow]] area.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1885  |title=Official Report of the Calcutta International Exhibition|author=|page=34 |ISBN=|passage=A collection of Calcutta '''chikan'''-work. Sent by Patna Chikanwala and Sons of Taltala, Calcutta}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999 |title=Embroidering Lives: Women's Work and Skill in the Lucknow Embroidery Industry|author=Clare M. Wilkinson-Weber |page= |ISBN=|passage=For many reasons, certain '''chikan''' stitches are ''not'' stem stiches or satin stitches —not even the technique is commensurate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004  |title=Travel House Guide To Incredible India |author=|page=74 |ISBN=|passage=In most markets, you will find amazing varieties in which '''chikan''' can be used: in bedspreads, napkins, curtains, cushion covers, besides sarees}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010 |title=Contractual Employment in Indian Labour Market: Emergence and Expansion |author=Neel Mani Prasad Verma, I. C. Awasthi |page=327 |ISBN=|passage=Dhaka was an important centre of '''chikan''' craft. It is said that Jahangir brought this craft to Lucknow and later it was whole-heartedly adopted by the Nawabs of Lucknow, thus it became a part of the culture of Lucknow.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011 |title=The Secret Diary of the World's Worst Cook|author=Subhadra Sen Gupta |page= |ISBN=|passage=You know I help women who do the '''chikan''' embroidery work?}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An item (such as a sari, bedspread, cushion cover, etc) that is made with chikan embroidery.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967 |title=Traveller in India - Volume 11 |author=|page=31 |ISBN=|passage=More and more people in India and abroad are getting familiar with the exquisite embroidered '''chikans''' of Lucknow, embroidered by the delicate and clever fingers of about 4000 ladies in and around the old city.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003 |title=The Chosen |author=Usha K. R. |page=231 |ISBN=|passage=Hetal's family sends her suitcasefuls of exquisite Lucknow '''chikans''', all in white.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008  |title=Looking Through Glass |author=Mukul Kesavan |page=324 |ISBN=|passage=That's why he wanted Ammi and Asharfi to sell the house and join him in Delhi, away from the nose-picking, Ganj-strolling, '''chikan'''-wearing, coffee-drinking habits of a lifetime.}}

